' Recovered from the baseline PNG's embedded PlantUML, then translated and corrected.
@startuml os_evolution
!include ../../../00_SUPPLEMENTARY/diagrams_common/skin.puml
title Evolution of Operating Systems

skinparam rectangle {
    roundCorner 10
}

' Generation 1
rectangle "**Gen 1: No OS**\n1945-1955" as gen1 #FFCDD2 {
    note as n1
    • Machine-code programming
    • One program at a time
    • Manual operation
    • CPU utilisation (provenance pending): ~30%
    end note
}

' Generation 2
rectangle "**Gen 2: Batch Processing**\n1955-1965" as gen2 #F8BBD9 {
    note as n2
    • GM-NAA I/O (1956)
    • Automatic transitions between jobs
    • CPU utilisation (provenance pending): ~90%
    • No interactivity
    end note
}

' Generation 3
rectangle "**Gen 3: Multiprogramming**\n1965-1980" as gen3 #E1BEE7 {
    note as n3
    • CTSS (1961), UNIX (1969)
    • Time-sharing
    • Multi-user
    • Interactivity!
    end note
}

' Generation 4
rectangle "**Gen 4: PC & GUI**\n1980-2000" as gen4 #C5CAE9 {
    note as n4
    • MS-DOS (1981)
    • Windows, Mac OS
    • Linux (1991)
    • GUI standard
    end note
}

' Generation 5
rectangle "**Gen 5: Mobile & Cloud**\n2000-present" as gen5 #BBDEFB {
    note as n5
    • iOS, Android
    • Cloud computing
    • Containers, K8s
    • Edge, IoT
    end note
}

gen1 -right-> gen2 : Automation
gen2 -right-> gen3 : Sharing
gen3 -down-> gen4 : Personal
gen4 -left-> gen5 : Mobile/Distributed

legend bottom
Historical periods overlap. The CTSS 1961 precursor predates the broad 1965-1980 period.
30% / 90% utilisation values are retained from the baseline;
measurement provenance or an explicitly adopted model remains an author decision.
endlegend
@enduml
